spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-74-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-75-## 6. User journeys
--
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-95-| FR-003 | The outbox survives reload and tab close. Reloading 
while offline shows the browser's normal offline error; when the app loads again, the pending items are shown and 
sent. Items of the current user appear in every tab of the same browser. | Must | AC-004 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-96-| FR-004 | Cross-tab ordering and ownership: the outbox order 
is shared by all tabs; per conversation, only the oldest unresolved item is eligible, and at most one tab holds the 
conversation's drain claim at a time (atomic claim). A claim is a lease: it ends when its tab closes or crashes, or 
when its holder exceeds the attempt deadline (request timeout 30 s; lease 45 s), and another tab then resumes after 
fencing (FR-006). When connectivity returns, an attempt that started before or during the outage is superseded 
immediately (fenced and redispatched under a new generation by whichever tab drains), so a hung holder never delays 
the D1 deadline. Across all tabs, no two create requests of the same conversation's outbox are in progress at once. | 
Must | AC-005, AC-041 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-97-| FR-005 | Cancel: an item can be cancelled from any tab only 
if it is known not to be stored and no earlier attempt can still commit (never dispatched, last attempt definitively 
rejected, or fenced as not stored) and it is not claimed; the cancel is an atomic transition against claims — a 
cancelled item is never dispatched afterwards; a dispatched item shows "enviando" without cancel. | Must | AC-006 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-98-| FR-006 | Attempt fencing: each dispatch is an attempt of the 
item's `clientMessageId`. Every create and fence request carries the item's attempt generation (monotonic per key). 
The server accepts a create only if its generation is the current one for that (sender, conversation, key) at the 
moment of commit, and a fence with generation g atomically either returns the stored message (item resolves as 
delivered) or makes every generation below g unable to commit — including requests still in the network that have not 
reached the server; a fence or create with a generation older than the server's current one is rejected. When an 
attempt ends without a usable response (network error, timeout, tab closed, lease expired, superseded, HTTP 5xx), the 
item is unknown; before any cancel, expiry, takeover, or new dispatch, the app fences it. The fence never creates a 
message. | Must | AC-038, AC-042, AC-045 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-99-| FR-007 | A definitive rejection (BR-005) stops automatic 
attempts for that item; it shows as failed with its text and actions "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; later items of the 
same conversation wait until it is resolved; other conversations are unaffected. For `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, 
"Tentar de novo" sends the same content under a new `clientMessageId`. | Must | AC-007, AC-008, AC-043, AC-046 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-100-| FR-008 | A rate-limit rejection is transient: the item stays 
pending and is retried automatically, preserving order and without duplicates. The insertion quota is charged only for 
a winning insertion (including among concurrent requests for the same key); replays that return an already-stored 
message do not consume it (D1). Independently, every create request (including replays, validation failures, and quota 
losers) consumes a per-user request budget, and fence/lookup calls consume their own per-user budget; exceeding any 
budget is a transient rate limit. | Must | AC-009 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:101:| FR-009 | An item not stored 24 h after the user pressed send 
is never sent automatically: once fenced as not stored, it shows as failed "expirada" with "Tentar de novo" and 
"Apagar"; an unknown item found stored resolves as delivered, never as expired. "Tentar de novo" on an expired item 
keeps the key and the original send time (for metrics) and starts a new 24 h delivery window, within which the normal 
transient/unknown rules apply. | Must | AC-010, AC-042 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-102-| FR-010 | Attachments/voice are never queued. A file whose 
upload fails offline keeps the existing per-file error and retry (send stays disabled until uploads finish); a message 
whose uploads succeeded but whose create request fails keeps the Phase 4 composer failure and "Tentar de novo". | Must 
| AC-011 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-103-| FR-011 | Identity fencing: each item belongs to an 
application user (`users.id`). Before every dispatch and fence call the app checks that the authenticated user equals 
the item's owner; every dispatch carries the expected sender, and the create and fence routes reject (without storing 
or fencing) a request whose authenticated user differs. When the authenticated user changes or signs out in any tab, 
all drains in all tabs stop; create, fence, and lookup responses that arrive afterwards are discarded: they never add 
content to another user's view, never resurrect deleted items, and never change a newer claim. Under the same 
identity, a response whose attempt generation is older than the item's current generation, or for an item already 
cancelled/deleted, is likewise ignored. | Must | AC-012, AC-044, AC-047 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:104:| FR-012 | Session end without explicit sign-out: an item is 
held (not failed, not deleted) only when the client's auth state reports the session ended (signed out, or refresh 
rejected); a 401 while the client still holds a valid session is treated as transient. Held items drain after the same 
user signs in again (subject to FR-009); another user on the browser never sees, sends, or deletes them. | Must | 
AC-012 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-105-| FR-013 | When the user clicks Sair at any in-app sign-out 
entry point with N > 0 unsent items, a dialog shows "Você tem N mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." 
with "Sair mesmo" and "Cancelar". Cancelar produces no logout request, success notice, or navigation. Sair mesmo stops 
drains in all tabs, deletes that user's outbox, and runs the existing sign-out (including the Presence logout sequence 
unchanged). With N = 0 sign-out is unchanged. | Must | AC-013 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-106-| FR-014 | Banner: "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas 
quando voltar" as soon as the browser reports offline; "Reconectando…" when any required messaging Realtime stream 
(messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list) has been unhealthy for more than 5 s while the browser is online; it 
disappears by itself once all are healthy. | Must | AC-014 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-107-| FR-015 | Every messaging Realtime stream 
(messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list, typing of the open conversation) has application-level health and 
recovery: after an error, timeout, or terminal closure it rejoins without user action using exponential backoff with a 
capped delay (in addition to the SDK's own retries); on recovery the messages and conversation-list streams trigger a 
catch-up refetch of the open feed and the list; the typing stream rejoins. | Must | AC-015 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-108-| FR-016 | Polling fallback: while any required stream has 
been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab is visible, the app fetches the open conversation's new messages, the 
receipt summaries of its loaded messages, any open reader-details list, and the conversation list every 15 s; on 
becoming visible while still unhealthy it fetches immediately, then every 15 s; no polling while hidden; once all 
streams are healthy no new polling request starts. Merging polling and Realtime results never duplicates a message and 
never replaces newer or enriched state (attachments, mentions, reactions, receipts, loaded history pages) with older 
data. | Must | AC-016, AC-048 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-109-| FR-017 | The typing indicator stays visible while the other 
person types continuously and disappears at most 5 s after they stop, blur the composer, send, leave the conversation, 
or close the tab. | Must | AC-017 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-110-| FR-018 | The indicator names typists: 1 → "Ana está 
digitando…", 2 → "Ana e Bruno estão digitando…", 3+ → "N pessoas estão digitando…", in direct, group, and room 
conversations. | Must | AC-018 |
--
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-113-| FR-021 | While connectivity and Realtime are healthy, within 
5 s and without reload, focus change, or reconnect: when the user reads messages (fully or partly) on one tab/device, 
their other tabs/devices show the same unread counts and read state, and the sender's receipt summary/details agree 
with the stored receipts on all of the sender's sessions. During an outage, FR-016 deadlines apply. | Must | AC-021 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-114-| FR-022 | While connectivity and Realtime are healthy, a 
message the user sends from one device/tab appears on their other devices/tabs within 5 s without reload; during an 
outage, FR-016 deadlines apply. | Must | AC-022 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-115-| FR-023 | In the composer, typing "@" opens a list of the 
conversation's current members except the user, filtered as they type, operable by mouse and keyboard (arrows, 
Enter/Tab, Esc); choosing inserts a highlighted mention. In a room the list contains only members of the room 
conversation. Typed "@name" text that was not chosen from the list is plain text. | Must | AC-023, AC-024 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-116-| FR-024 | On first creation the server stores mentions in the 
same transaction as the message, as application user IDs of conversation members at that time other than the sender; 
other requested IDs create no mention. The original submitted composition (content, reply target, requested mention 
IDs) is what replays are compared against: an unchanged replay returns the stored message without re-validating 
recipients; a different composition under the same key returns `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`. Clients cannot write 
mention records directly; only conversation members can read them. | Must | AC-024, AC-046 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-117-| FR-025 | With the Phase 4 desktop-notification toggle on and 
permission granted, a mentioned user receives one desktop notification for a message that mentions them (direct, 
group, room, thread reply), titled "<Autor> mencionou você em <conversa>" with the full text; clicking opens the 
conversation. Before emitting, the app confirms with authoritative data, under the current identity, that the user is 
still a member and (for rooms) mentioned; then, with no further awaits before display, it re-checks that the same user 
is still signed in and that no removal signal for that conversation has arrived; otherwise the pending notification is 
discarded, as it is on any account switch or sign-out. Residual race accepted (D6): a removal that the app has not yet 
received when the final check runs may let that one notification show. Suppression is browser-wide: no tab notifies if 
any of the user's tabs in that browser is visible and viewing that conversation, regardless of which tab receives the 
event first. | Must | AC-025, AC-026, AC-049 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-118-| FR-026 | Mentions render distinctly for everyone; a message 
that mentions the viewing user is highlighted for that user with a non-color cue. | Must | AC-027 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:119:| FR-027 | The client records metric events — send latency 
(press → server confirmed), outbox wait time, send outcomes by reason, Realtime stream drops/recoveries and outage 
duration, polling activations and duration — in a bounded buffer (oldest dropped when full) and sends them 
fire-and-forget in batches of at most 50 events and 16 KB, at most one delivery in flight, each aborted after 5 s, 
dropping batches under overload. The authenticated, per-user rate-limited route rejects oversized requests, accepts 
only closed event/reason enums and bounded numeric fields, drops anything else, adds a server-derived app version, and 
writes structured log lines under BR-013. | Must | AC-028 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:120:| FR-028 | Metrics never block, slow, or break messaging: 
messaging code never awaits metric delivery; an erroring, hanging, or overloaded metrics route changes no messaging 
behavior or timing and shows no error. | Must | AC-029 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-121-| FR-029 | `mark_conversation_read` refuses to mark a 
conversation for a user who is not a member of it. | Must | AC-030 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-122-| FR-030 | `get_unread_counts` is not executable by the `anon` 
role; authenticated use keeps working. | Must | AC-031 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-123-| FR-031 | After a page reload, if the drawer was open, it 
reopens on the same conversation for the same user on the same browser; if that conversation is no longer accessible, 
the conversation list opens without an error; a closed drawer stays closed. | Must | AC-032 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-124-| FR-032 | Existing messaging behavior (Phase 4 flows: 
receipts, attachments, voice notes, stars, DM/group notifications and their once-across-tabs claim, failed-send retry 
for attachment messages, reconnect catch-up) does not regress. | Must | AC-033 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-125-| FR-033 | When the outbox cannot be written (site storage 
unavailable or the write fails), nothing is dispatched; the text and reply target stay in the composer with the 
message "Este navegador não permite guardar mensagens; libere o armazenamento do site" (D5). | Must | AC-039 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-126-| FR-034 | Key-based correlation: the sender's representations 
of a message (outbox item, create and fence responses, feed fetches, Realtime events) carry its `clientMessageId`, and 
the feed matches pending items to stored messages only by that key, so each message shows as exactly one bubble in 
every sender tab, including identical texts and Realtime arriving before the HTTP response. | Must | AC-050 |
--
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-138-| BR-007 | Cancel only for items known not to be stored, with 
no earlier attempt able to commit, and not claimed; a cancelled item is removed in all tabs and never dispatched. | 
R1-q5 B, R2-q7 A; reviews r2 #2, #5, r3 #1 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-139-| BR-008 | An explicit user sign-out with unsent items 
requires confirmation; confirming deletes that user's outbox; a sign-out not initiated by the user keeps it. 
Cancelling has no side effect. | R2-q5 A, R2-q6 A |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-140-| BR-009 | Typing: only current conversation members join and 
send on a conversation's typing topic; a user's own typing (any session) is never shown to them; an indicator expires 
≤ 5 s after the last signal; the app leaves a conversation's topic within 5 s of the user's removal; residual exposure 
(receive and send) of a tampered removed-member client is bounded by access-token lifetime (D3, D6). | R1-q8 A, D, E; 
R1-q16 E; D3; D6 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-141-| BR-010 | Mentions: only current members of the conversation 
at first creation, other than the sender, can be mentioned (rooms: members of the room conversation); no 
`@todos`/`@here`. | R1-q10 A, E; R2-q10 A; D4 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-142-| BR-011 | Desktop-notification eligibility (extends Phase 4): 
toggle on and permission granted; never for the user's own message; the same user must still be signed in and a member 
at emission time (final check without intervening awaits; residual race accepted, D6); never while any tab of the user 
in that browser is visible and viewing that conversation; DM/group: every new message; room: only messages that 
mention the user; at most one notification per message per browser. | R1-q10 B, R1-q16 C/F, R2-q8 A, R2-q9 A–C; 
reviews r2 #9, r3 #3 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-143-| BR-012 | Polling runs only while a required Realtime stream 
has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab is visible, every 15 s (immediately on becoming visible), and no new 
poll starts once all streams are healthy. | R1-q7 A, R1-q16 D, R2-q12 C |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:144:| BR-013 | Metric log lines contain only closed-enum event 
type and outcome/reason, bounded durations/counts, conversation type, timestamp, and server-derived app version — 
never message text, attachment names, conversation names, user names or emails, user/conversation identifiers, or any 
client-supplied free text. | R1-q12 B, R2-q2, confirmation point 9; review r2 #14 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-145-| BR-014 | Database rollout: migrations stay local during the 
phase (written + applied locally with recorded rollback SQL); at phase end they are applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` 
only with explicit owner authorization, followed by catalog readback and a runtime smoke check; the application is 
deployed by the owner after the migrations; rollback is app first, then migrations. | R1-q13 A |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-146-| BR-015 | Tests: only integration (real local 
Supabase/Realtime/API routes) and E2E (real UI, Playwright); no new unit tests; a touched unit test is replaced by 
integration/E2E coverage and deleted, recorded in TRACK. Each new E2E spec counts only after 3 consecutive passing 
runs. | AGENTS.md test policy, R2-q13 A |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-147-| BR-016 | Conversation restore is per browser and per user; a 
different user never inherits it. | R2-q11 A, B, D |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-148-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-149-## 9. Data and integrations
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-150-
--
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-153-- Browser connectivity events, Realtime stream status, tab 
visibility, Supabase auth state changes.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-154-- Responses from `POST /api/messages/create` (status and error 
code) and from the fence/lookup call.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-155-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-156-### Data outputs
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-157-- Browser IndexedDB outbox keyed by application user ID: 
`clientMessageId`, conversation ID, text, reply target, requested mention user IDs, pressed-send time, delivery-window 
start, order, state (waiting / claimed / unknown / failed + reason / expired), attempt generation, claim holder and 
lease expiry. Deleted per BR-007/BR-008; never shared across users.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-158-- Database: attempt-generation state per (sender, 
conversation, `clientMessageId`) so a fenced or stale generation cannot commit; the original submitted composition 
used for replay comparison; mention records written atomically with the message; private typing-topic authorization 
(RLS on `realtime.messages`, `users.supabase_uid = auth.uid()::text` → `users.id` membership); 
`mark_conversation_read` with an internal membership check; `get_unread_counts` without `anon` EXECUTE; rate-limit 
buckets for all create requests and for fence/lookup calls, separate from the insertion quota.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:159:- API: create route sender and attempt-generation fencing, 
winning-insert quota charging, request budget, `clientMessageId` in responses; fence/lookup route for the caller's own 
message only; authenticated, rate-limited, size-bounded metrics route.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:160:- Structured metric log lines (BR-013).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-161-- Persisted drawer state per user: open flag + active 
conversation ID.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-162-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-163-### Integrations
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-164-- Supabase Postgres, Realtime (`postgres_changes`, private 
broadcast topics), Auth (server routes use `createSupabaseServerClient` + `auth.getUser()`).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-165-- Browser APIs: IndexedDB, Web Locks and/or BroadcastChannel 
for cross-tab claims and signals, Notification API (Phase 4), Page Visibility.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:166:- Hosting/Supabase logs as the metrics sink; no new external 
analytics dependency.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-167-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-168-## 10. Constraints and assumptions
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-169-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-170-### Constraints
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-171-- Next.js App Router, TypeScript strict, Supabase, TanStack 
Query; reuse existing hooks, repositories, routes, and query keys rather than parallel implementations.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-172-- Identity: RLS compares `users.supabase_uid` with 
`auth.uid()::text`; foreign keys use `users.id`; messages link to rooms through `conversations.room_id`.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-173-- Browsers: Chrome and Edge desktop (automated E2E); Firefox 
desktop (verified). Safari and mobile not required.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-174-- No service worker; background notifications are page-level 
only.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:175:- No message text, names, or identifiers in metrics (BR-013); 
no external analytics service.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-176-- The sign-out confirmation must not change the Presence 
logout sequence (presence-safety rules apply to that touch point).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-177-- Online database changes only at phase end with explicit 
authorization (BR-014); the hosted app currently runs the pre-Phase-4 build until the owner deploys, so new server 
contracts must stay compatible with that build and with the Phase 4 build until deployment: unkeyed legacy message 
writes and existing RPC/route signatures keep working against the migrated database.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-178-- Repository test policy (BR-015); type-check, lint, and build 
must pass.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:179:- Timing parameters chosen under the owner's delegation (D4) 
and adjustable in planning only with owner approval: claim request timeout 30 s, lease 45 s, metrics batch ≤ 50 events 
/ 16 KB, one metrics delivery in flight, 5 s metrics abort.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-180-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-181-### Assumptions
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-182-- None beyond the user-confirmed answers and owner decisions 
recorded in `state.md`.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-183-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-184-## 11. Edge cases and failure modes
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-185-
--
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-214-| Anon, outsider, public-topic, or removed-member typing 
access | Refused / no events; removed member's app leaves at once; tampered client bounded by D3 (AC-019). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-215-| Forged mention IDs, plain "@name", changed mentions on 
replay, recipient removed before replay | No mention for non-members/self/plain text; changed composition → 409; 
unchanged replay returns the stored message (AC-024, AC-046). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-216-| Recipient removed or account switched after commit but 
before the notification is shown | No notification once the app has the removal/switch signal; a removal not yet 
received at the final check is the accepted D6 race (AC-049). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-217-| Manual retry of an expired item hits 429 or an unknown 
outcome | New 24 h window: retried/fenced automatically (AC-010). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-218-| Partial reads by others during a Realtime-only outage | 
Receipt summaries and open reader details update via polling (AC-016). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-219-| Hidden tab receives a mention event first while another tab 
views that conversation | No notification (AC-026). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:220:| Malicious, oversized, hanging, or sustained metrics traffic 
| Rejected or dropped; route rate-limited; messaging unaffected (AC-028, AC-029). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-221-| Restored conversation no longer accessible | Conversation 
list opens without error (AC-032). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-222-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-223-## 12. Security, privacy, compliance, and abuse considerations
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-224-- Outbox content lives only in the user's browser, scoped to 
the application user ID, fenced against account switches on the client and on the server (expected-sender check on 
create and fence), and deleted on confirmed explicit sign-out.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-225-- The fence/lookup call acts only on the caller's own message 
for that key, never creates a message, and is rejected for anon users and for other users' keys.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-226-- Every create request consumes a per-user request budget and 
fence/lookup calls their own, so the insertion-quota exemption for replays (D1) cannot be used for unlimited traffic.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-227-- Typing topics are private and members-only for join and send 
(Realtime RLS with `users.supabase_uid = auth.uid()::text` → `users.id`); the app leaves within 5 s of a removal; 
residual receive/send exposure of tampered removed-member clients accepted (D3, D6).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-228-- Mentions are validated and stored server-side in the message 
transaction; clients cannot write them directly; only members can read them; notifications are re-authorized right 
before emission (residual race accepted, D6).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-229-- Notifications never leak content of conversations the user 
is not (or no longer) a member of.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:230:- Metrics: closed enums, bounded numbers and sizes, 
server-derived version, per-user rate limit, no free text (BR-013); unauthenticated requests are rejected and produce 
no metric log line.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-231-- `mark_conversation_read` membership check and `anon` revoke 
on `get_unread_counts` close two Phase 4 findings.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-232-- No service-role key in client code; service-role use never 
replaces application authorization checks.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-233-- Every migration, RLS/Realtime policy, repository, and API 
route change passes the Supabase/RLS review gate.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-234-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-235-## 13. Accessibility, localization, and usability 
considerations
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-236-- UI copy in Brazilian Portuguese. The banner text, sign-out 
dialog text, typing phrases, and mention notification title in this SPEC are required; item-state labels ("pendente", 
"enviando", "expirada"), actions ("Cancelar", "Tentar de novo", "Apagar"), are proposed (the storage message in FR-033 
is the owner's D5 wording), and equivalent Portuguese wording with the same meaning is accepted (D4).
--
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-247-- AC-004 (FR-003): Given A has a pending item, when A reloads 
while offline, then the browser's offline error is shown; when the network returns and the app loads again, the item 
is shown as pending and is delivered exactly once. Given A closes the tab with a pending item, then reopening the app 
later delivers it once.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-248-- AC-005 (FR-004, BR-003, BR-004; must-not R1-q16 B): Given A 
has two tabs and queued m1, m2 offline in conversation X in tab 1, then both appear once in tab 2; when both tabs come 
back online simultaneously and m1's real create request is held, then neither tab sends a create request for m2 until 
m1 is resolved; the network logs of both tabs show no overlapping create requests for X; B receives m1 then m2, 
exactly once each; the database holds one row per key.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-249-- AC-006 (FR-005, BR-007): Given a waiting item never 
dispatched, when A cancels it in tab 2 at the moment tab 1 tries to claim it, then either the cancel wins (the item 
disappears from both tabs and no create request for it is ever sent) or the claim wins (no cancel action is offered 
and the item is delivered once); given a dispatched item, it shows "enviando" and offers no cancel.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-250-- AC-007 (FR-007, BR-003, BR-005): Given A queued m1 and m2 in 
room conversation X and m3 in DM Y, and A lost access to X before reconnecting, when connectivity returns, then m1 
shows failed with its text and "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar", m2 stays waiting with no create request sent, and m3 is 
delivered.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-251-- AC-008 (FR-007, BR-003): Given AC-007's state, when A 
regains access to X and clicks "Tentar de novo" on m1, then m1 and then m2 are delivered in order; given instead A 
clicks "Apagar" on m1, then m1 is never delivered and m2 is attempted next.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-252-- AC-009 (FR-002, FR-008, BR-005): Given A's create quota is 
exhausted when A's 12 queued items start draining, then rejected items stay pending and retry automatically, and B 
finally sees all 12 in order, once each. On the real route: a replay of an already-stored message does not reduce the 
insertion quota; replays, validation failures, and quota losers all consume the request budget and are rejected (429) 
once it is exhausted; fence/lookup calls are limited by their own budget; concurrent requests for one new key charge 
the insertion quota once; concurrent distinct keys at the quota boundary store no more messages than the quota allows.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:253:- AC-010 (FR-009, BR-006): Given a never-dispatched item whose 
send time is more than 24 h ago, when connectivity returns, then no create request is sent for it and it shows 
"expirada" with "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar"; "Tentar de novo" delivers it once; given a manual retry of an expired 
item that gets 429 and then an unknown outcome, it is retried/fenced automatically and stored once.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-254-- AC-011 (FR-010, BR-002): Given A is offline, when A adds a 
file, then its upload shows the existing per-file error and retry, Send stays disabled, and nothing enters the outbox; 
given A's uploads succeeded and then the network drops, when A sends, then the message stays in the composer with the 
Phase 4 failure and "Tentar de novo", and nothing enters the outbox.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-255-- AC-012 (FR-011, FR-012, BR-001, BR-005; must-not R1-q16 A): 
Given A has unsent items and A's browser session ends without Sair (auth cookies/storage cleared or refresh token 
revoked so the client reports signed out), when C signs in on the same browser, then C sees none of A's items and no 
create or fence request is sent while C is authenticated; when A signs in again, A's items (< 24 h) are delivered as 
A. Given a 401 while A's client session is valid, then the item stays pending and is retried.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-256-- AC-013 (FR-013, BR-008): Given A has 2 unsent items, when A 
clicks Sair from each in-app entry point, then the dialog shows "Você tem 2 mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas 
mensagens."; Cancelar sends no logout request, shows no notice, and does not navigate; Sair mesmo with a drain in 
flight in another tab stops all drains, deletes the outbox, and signs out with the Presence logout sequence as before; 
after A signs in again the outbox is empty. Given 0 unsent items, Sair signs out without a dialog.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-257-- AC-014 (FR-014): Given the drawer is open, when the browser 
goes offline, then "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar" appears immediately; when only Realtime 
drops for 3 s, no banner appears; when the messages stream alone, and separately the conversation-list stream alone, 
stays unhealthy for more than 5 s, "Reconectando…" appears; when all recover, the banner disappears without user 
action.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-258-- AC-015 (FR-015): Given each required stream is dropped 
independently (messages stream only; conversation-list stream only, including a terminal closure; typing stream only), 
when Realtime becomes available again, then that stream recovers without user action; B's messages sent during the 
outage appear once; unread counts are correct; typing works again; recorded recovery events show increasing, capped 
retry delays.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-259-- AC-016 (FR-016, BR-012; must-not R1-q16 D): Given Realtime 
is blocked while HTTP works and A's tab is visible, when B sends a message at the outage start, then A sees it within 
45 s; a message B sends after polling started appears within 15 s plus request time; the list's unread counts update; 
when C reads part of A's earlier messages during the outage (no new message sent), A's receipt summaries and open 
reader-details list update within 15 s plus request time once polling is active. Given only the conversation-list 
stream is unhealthy for more than 30 s, polling starts too. When A's tab is hidden, no polling requests are made; when 
it becomes visible, a fetch happens immediately. When Realtime recovers while a poll is in flight, the message 
delivered by both appears once, and no new polling request starts during the next 60 s.
--
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-265-- AC-022 (FR-022): Given healthy connectivity and A on two 
devices, when A sends on device 1, then it appears once on device 2 within 5 s without reload.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-266-- AC-023 (FR-023, BR-010): Given A types "@" in a room 
conversation, then the list shows only room-conversation members other than A, filters as A types, and supports 
arrows/Enter/Tab/Esc and mouse; choosing "Bruno" inserts a highlighted mention; a workspace user who never joined the 
room chat is not listed.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-267-- AC-024 (FR-023, FR-024, BR-010; must-not R1-q16 F): On the 
real route and local DB: a message mentioning a non-member ID and the sender's own ID stores no mention for either and 
notifies neither; a replay that changes only the requested mention IDs returns `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` and changes 
nothing; a message whose text contains "@Bruno" typed without selection stores no mention; a forced failure of mention 
storage stores neither the message nor any mention; anon and non-members cannot read mention records; no client can 
insert/update/delete them directly.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-268-- AC-025 (FR-025, BR-011): Given B has notifications enabled 
and permission granted, B's tab hidden or viewing another conversation, when A mentions B in a room, then B receives 
exactly one notification "<A> mencionou você em <sala>" with the full text; clicking opens that room in the drawer; a 
room message without a mention of B produces none.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-269-- AC-026 (FR-025, BR-011; must-not R1-q16 C): Given B has 
notifications enabled, then a DM mention yields exactly one notification; a thread-reply mention yields one; with B's 
two tabs where the hidden tab receives the event first and the visible tab is viewing that conversation, none; A's own 
message yields none for A; with the toggle off, none; a mention across B's two hidden tabs yields exactly one.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-270-- AC-027 (FR-026): Given A's message mentions B, then all 
members see the mention styled distinctly, and only B sees the whole message highlighted with a non-color cue.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:271:- AC-028 (FR-027, BR-013): Given a send, a stream recovery, 
and a polling episode occur, then the metrics route writes structured lines with enum event type/reason, durations, 
and server-derived version; a scan finds no message text, names, emails, or user/conversation IDs; a payload with 
unknown fields, free-text strings, out-of-range numbers, or a forged version produces no such values in the logs; a 
request over 50 events or 16 KB is rejected; requests beyond the per-user rate limit are rejected; an unauthenticated 
request is rejected and produces no metric log line.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:272:- AC-029 (FR-028): Given the metrics route errors, and 
separately hangs, and separately is flooded with sustained traffic, when A sends, goes offline, and reconnects, then 
AC-001/AC-002 behavior and timing hold, at most one metrics request is in flight from A's tab, a hanging metrics 
request is aborted after 5 s, the client buffer never exceeds its bound (oldest events dropped), and no error is shown.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-273-- AC-030 (FR-029): Given user E is not a member of X, when 
`mark_conversation_read` is invoked for E and X through every role that can execute it, then the call is refused and 
E's read state for X is unchanged (integration, local DB).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-274-- AC-031 (FR-030): Given the `anon` role, when it calls 
`get_unread_counts`, then permission is denied; an authenticated member still gets correct counts (integration, local 
DB).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-275-- AC-032 (FR-031, BR-016): Given the drawer open on X, when A 
reloads, then it reopens on X; given A lost access to X, then the list opens with no error; given C signs in on that 
browser, then C does not get A's conversation; given the drawer was closed, it stays closed.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-276-- AC-033 (FR-032): The existing Phase 4 messaging E2E 
regression suite and DB integration suite pass after each slice; `npm run type-check`, `npm run lint`, and `npm run 
build` pass.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-277-- AC-034 (BR-014, §10): Every migration of the phase is 
applied locally with recorded rollback SQL; before any online application, the currently hosted build (git ref 
confirmed by the owner) and the Phase 4 build each pass a send/read/unread smoke against the migrated local database 
(legacy unkeyed writes still work); at phase end, after explicit owner authorization, it is applied to 
`vhabpcoyypobgasacsko`, the catalog is read back from that target, and a runtime smoke check passes; the hand-off 
states the app is ready to deploy after the migrations.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:278:- AC-035 (§12): The Supabase/RLS review gate passes for every 
migration, RLS/Realtime policy, repository, and API route change.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-279-- AC-036 (BR-015): Each new E2E spec passed 3 consecutive runs 
in Chrome and in Edge; the key flows (outbox, banner, typing, mentions) were verified in Firefox and recorded; no unit 
test was added; every touched unit test was replaced and deleted, recorded in TRACK.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-280-- AC-037 (FR-014, §13): The banner is announced via a polite 
live region, item states have text labels, and the sign-out dialog and mention list are fully keyboard-operable.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-281-- AC-038 (FR-002, FR-006, BR-004): Given A's create request is 
stored but its response is dropped, then the item becomes unknown, the fence resolves it as delivered without a second 
create, and B sees it once.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-282-- AC-039 (FR-033, R1-q2 A, D5): Given IndexedDB is unavailable 
(and separately a failing outbox write), when A sends a text message, then zero create requests are sent, the text and 
reply target stay in the composer, and "Este navegador não permite guardar mensagens; libere o armazenamento do site" 
is shown.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-283-- AC-040 (FR-001): Given A is online and the create request is 
held, when A closes the tab before the server confirms, then on reopening the app the item is fenced and B sees the 
message exactly once.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-284-- AC-041 (FR-004): Given tab 1 holds the claim and its create 
request is held, then tab 2 sends no create request for that conversation while tab 1's lease is live; when tab 1 is 
closed (and separately when its lease expires while it hangs), tab 2 fences and the message is stored once. Given tab 
1's attempt hangs across an outage, when connectivity returns with that single item pending and quota available, then 
it is delivered within 10 s (D1) and stored once.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:285:- AC-042 (FR-006, FR-009): Given an unknown item whose request 
was actually stored, when A tries to cancel it or its 24 h passes, then no cancel is offered before fencing, the fence 
resolves it as delivered, and it never shows "expirada".
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-286-- AC-043 (FR-007): Given an item rejected with 
`CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, when A clicks "Tentar de novo", then its content is stored once under a new key and the 
conflicting stored message is unchanged.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-287-- AC-044 (FR-011, BR-001, §12): Given A's drain is paused 
before dispatch and the browser's session switches to C (a member of the same conversation), when the drain would 
resume, then no create or fence request is sent as C; a crafted create or fence request carrying A as expected sender 
with C's session is rejected and stores or fences nothing; an anon fence/lookup is rejected; C fencing or looking up a 
key that belongs to A's message (with C as sender) neither returns A's message nor affects A's attempts.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-288-- AC-045 (FR-006, BR-004): With real local Postgres and the 
real routes: (a) attempt 1's INSERT is blocked (row lock held by the test) while a fence for that key reports not 
stored; on release attempt 1 does not commit; (b) attempt 1's request is held before reaching the server while a fence 
runs; when released it is rejected as stale; (c) a stale fence (older generation) arriving after a newer attempt was 
dispatched is rejected and the newer attempt commits; in every case at most one message is stored (integration + E2E).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-289-- AC-046 (FR-007, FR-024, BR-005): On the real route: 403 
(missing conversation or not a participant), 400 (invalid reply target), and 413 (content too large) are classified 
definitive in the drawer (failed with text); a 5xx returned after the request reached the server is classified unknown 
and fenced; an unchanged replay after a mentioned recipient left the conversation returns the stored message (not 409) 
and notifies no removed recipient.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-290-- AC-047 (FR-011, FR-013): Given a real create response and a 
real fence response held while the browser switches from A to C, and separately while A confirms Sair mesmo, and 
separately (same identity) while the item is cancelled or superseded by a newer generation, when the responses are 
released, then no A content appears in C's view, no deleted or cancelled item reappears, and no newer claim or attempt 
is changed.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-291-- AC-048 (FR-016): Given a poll response is held while 
Realtime delivers a newer state (a reaction, a receipt, an attachment message, a mention) and older history pages are 
loaded, when the stale poll is released, then the feed keeps the newer state, the loaded pages, and one bubble per 
message.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-292-- AC-049 (FR-025, BR-011; must-not R1-q16 F): Given B is 
mentioned and B's authoritative check has already returned "member" but display is held, when B's app receives B's 
removal signal (and separately when the browser switches to another account) before display, then no notification is 
shown; given the check is held before it runs and B is removed, then no notification is shown.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-293-- AC-050 (FR-034): Given A sends two messages with identical 
text (one plain, one replying to a message) and Realtime delivers each before its HTTP response, then each shows as 
exactly one bubble in both of A's tabs throughout, matched by `clientMessageId`.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-294-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-295-## 15. Test strategy
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-296-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-297-- Test levels:
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:298:  - Integration (local Supabase Postgres/Realtime and real API 
routes): sender and attempt-generation fencing incl. stale requests and stale fences (AC-044, AC-045), insertion 
quota, request budget, and fence budget (AC-009), fence/lookup authorization incl. anon and other users' keys (AC-038, 
AC-042, AC-044), outcome classes (AC-046), mention atomicity/validation/replay/RLS (AC-024, AC-046), private 
typing-topic authorization incl. anon/outsider/public topic (AC-019), `mark_conversation_read` membership (AC-030), 
`get_unread_counts` anon revoke (AC-031), metrics route auth, enums, sizes, rate limit, redaction (AC-028).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-299-  - E2E (Playwright, real UI; two or more real accounts; two 
tabs of one account; a second device as a separate browser context): every other AC. Network shaping only — the app, 
routes, and Supabase stay real: browser-context offline mode; blocking only the Realtime WebSocket or a single stream; 
holding a request or response by delayed pass-through to the real server; dropping a response or replacing it with a 
gateway 5xx after pass-through; disabling IndexedDB or failing its writes in the page (AC-039). Old-build 
compatibility: the hosted and Phase 4 builds are run locally against the migrated database (AC-034). Expiry may use a 
test-controlled clock without changing the product rule.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-300-  - Unit: none (BR-015).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-301-- Tests trace to acceptance criteria by AC ID; written from 
the ACs before or with the implementation.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-302-- Test data: accounts A, B, C, D, E in one company; a DM, a 
group, and a room conversation; a room the test can remove A/E from and re-add; a way to exhaust A's create quota 
locally.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-303-- Environments: local Supabase for integration/E2E; Chrome and 
Edge for automated E2E; Firefox verification of key flows recorded; online target only for phase-end readback and 
smoke (BR-014).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-304-- Reliability: each new E2E spec counts only after 3 
consecutive passing runs (BR-015).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:305:- Mandatory failure-path tests: every AC-003..AC-013 and 
AC-038..AC-050 scenario, short vs long and per-stream Realtime drops, hidden-tab polling, polling/Realtime overlap and 
stale polls, typing tab close and conversation switch, typing access by anon/outsider/removed member, 
forged/plain/changed mentions, visible+hidden tab notification race, delayed notification authorization, 
malicious/oversized/hanging/sustained metrics traffic, restore with lost access.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md-306-
